DECK_TITLES['Boot+of+a+system'] = 'Boot of a system'
TOPICS['Boot+of+a+system'] = 'Reset, runtime memory initialization, vendor startup code, and bootloader handover.'
REVIEWED['Boot+of+a+system'] = list(range(1,35))
TITLES['Boot+of+a+system'] = {1:'Embedded Systems — Boot'}
COMMENTS['Boot+of+a+system'] = {
5: 'The common idea is the transition from reset hardware to the first firmware instruction. “BIOS” names the PC example; the MCU example on the following pages instead begins at a reset handler and can run a bare-metal application without a BIOS or operating system.',
7: 'BSS is a reservation in the runtime memory layout, rather than a block of arbitrary initial bytes that must be stored in flash. Compare pages 14–15: DATA receives initial values copied from flash, whereas BSS is created by zero-filling its RAM range. “Non-initialized” here means no explicit nonzero initializer, not an undefined initial value for static-storage objects.',
9: 'Apparent address error: the slide gives `0x80000000` as a general Cortex-M vector base. Cortex-M0 fetches vectors at `0x00000000`; Cortex-M4 also starts there and can relocate the table using VTOR, whose offset is measured from zero. Vendor memory aliasing determines which physical memory appears there. See [Arm’s Cortex-M introduction, p. 14](https://community.arm.com/cfs-file/__key/telligent-evolution-components-attachments/01-2057-00-00-00-01-28-35/Cortex_2D00_M-for-Beginners-_2D00_-2017_5F00_EN_5F00_v2.pdf) and [M4 guide, §4.3.4](https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5f2ac4ab60a93e65927bbdbf).',
10: 'Architecture qualification: on Cortex-M, hardware already loads the initial MSP from vector entry 0 before executing the reset handler. The explicit stack-pointer assignment in the later code re-establishes that value. The stack pointers are MSP and PSP; “SSP” appears to be a terminology slip here. See [Arm’s startup-file explanation](https://developer.arm.com/community/arm-community-blogs/b/architectures-and-processors-blog/posts/decoding-the-startup-file-for-arm-cortex-m4).',
18: 'This is an abbreviated vector-table illustration. Real Cortex-M tables include reserved slots at architecture-defined offsets; deleting those slots would shift every subsequent handler to the wrong exception number. Treat the displayed sequence as an explanation of the entries, rather than a complete table to copy into a project. See [Arm’s vector-table layout, §2.3.4](https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5f2ac4ab60a93e65927bbdbf).',
20: 'LMA is the load-memory address of the initial bytes in flash; VMA is the address used while the program runs in RAM. With an 8-byte DATA section, the loop copies offsets 4 and then 0. The linker symbols provide addresses and bounds; they are not ordinary variables containing the application data.',
21: 'Apparent heading error: although the title says “DATA section,” the symbols `__bss_start__`/`__bss_end__` and stores of zero show BSS initialization. It is the zero-fill counterpart of the copy operation on the preceding page.',
25: 'The excerpt places the default-handler definition after the `SystemInit` call. Read it as selected fragments: if execution literally fell through into `Default_Handler`, the `b .` instruction would loop forever instead of reaching the later memory-initialization code. The complete startup file must provide the intended continuation.',
26: 'Each copy-table entry is three 32-bit words: flash source address, RAM destination address, and byte count. The outer loop advances by 12 bytes to the next entry; the inner loop copies that section in 4-byte units. This handles several RAM regions using linker-generated descriptors instead of one hard-coded DATA range.',
28: 'Here `blx r0` is a function call to `main`, with a return address; this differs from the blanket “jump, not a function call” wording on page 10. The following `b .` deliberately traps execution if `main` returns. The assembly shown on each page determines which transfer is actually used.',
32: 'The final two bullets describe alternative hardware capabilities, not two simultaneous requirements. Follow page 33 when an IVT relocation register is available; follow page 34 when the device instead supports the illustrated memory-remapping mechanism.',
}
ISSUES['Boot+of+a+system'] = 'Comments flag vector-base addresses, stack-pointer terminology, abbreviated vector entries, the BSS heading, and startup excerpt/control-flow ambiguities.'

DECK_TITLES['Microcontrollers'] = 'Microcontrollers'
TOPICS['Microcontrollers'] = 'MCU organization, clock trees, memory maps, GPIO, analog peripherals, timers, and SPI/UART/I2C.'
REVIEWED['Microcontrollers'] = list(range(1,81))
TITLES['Microcontrollers'] = {1:'Embedded Systems — Microcontrollers',12:'Microcontroller subsystems — Clock',20:'Microcontroller subsystems — Memory',29:'Microcontroller subsystems — GPIO',37:'Microcontroller subsystems — Analog comparators',44:'Microcontroller subsystems — Analog to Digital Converter',51:'Microcontroller subsystems — Real Time Clock',62:'Communication — SPI Bus',68:'Communication — UART',73:'Communication — I2C'}
COMMENTS['Microcontrollers'] = {
4: 'The numeric ranges on this and page 2 characterize the lecture’s 2020–2021 examples. The architectural distinction being illustrated is integration: an MCU combines its processing core, small memories, and peripherals on one chip, as the next diagram shows. Do not use these ranges as universal selection limits.',
13: r'The RTC frequency is convenient because $32768=2^{15}$: fifteen divide-by-two stages yield a 1 Hz tick. A separate slow clock can maintain time while the faster core clock is stopped, provided the RTC clock domain remains powered.',
16: r'“ppm” means parts per million of fractional frequency error. A constant error of 20 ppm gives approximately $20\times10^{-6}\times86400=1.728$ seconds of time error per day. The same percentage error can be acceptable for a timeout and unacceptable for long-term calendar timekeeping.',
18: 'Read the clock tree from left to right. Multiplexers select a source; the PLL changes frequency; dividers reduce it; enable-controlled gates stop clock delivery to individual blocks. Selecting a working source and enabling a peripheral’s gate are separate configuration steps.',
24: 'The left column is an address-space map, not a picture of the amount of memory physically installed. The expanded views identify implemented regions and reserved holes. Notice the flash region at `0x08000000` and the boot-dependent mapping at zero; these help interpret the address ambiguity in [[University/Embedded Systems/Notes/2 From processors to microcontrollers-20261026/Boot of a system#Slide 9 — Microcontroller boot sequence|Boot, page 9]].',
28: 'PPAGE selects which part of global flash appears in the fixed 16 KB local window. The same local address inside that window can therefore refer to different physical bytes after PPAGE changes. The unpaged regions stay directly accessible, which is useful for code that must remain reachable while a bank is switched.',
31: 'The blue overlay marks the active path for this configuration; the other wires show alternative uses of the same pin. Across pages 31–35, follow that overlay to distinguish analog routing, a sampled digital input, software-driven output, and output driven directly by a peripheral.',
43: r'“Front” means an edge of the digital comparator output. Counting measures how many such edges occur; capture latches the timer count at an edge. If two captures differ by $\Delta C$ ticks at timer frequency $f_t$, the interval is $\Delta t=\Delta C/f_t$, with overflow accounting when needed.',
46: 'Terminology ambiguity: a fixed reference and a ratiometric measurement are different ways to manage supply dependence. In a ratiometric arrangement the sensor excitation and ADC reference track the same supply, so their ratio stays stable. A fixed reference instead anchors the conversion scale independently. See [Analog Devices’ ratiometric measurement explanation](https://www.analog.com/en/resources/analog-dialogue/articles/2021/05/21/14/24/how-to-select-and-design-the-best-rtd-temperature-sensing-system.html).',
52: r'The annual drift follows directly from fractional frequency error: $5\times10^{-6}\times365\times24\times3600=157.68$ seconds. This assumes a constant 5 ppm offset; temperature-dependent changes can alter both the magnitude and direction of the actual accumulated error.',
56: 'Apparent label mismatch: the right-hand picture is captioned “Single shot,” but its internal labels show two capture events and a time difference, matching page 55’s capture diagram. Use the picture for interval capture; it does not illustrate a one-shot timer stopping at a programmed terminal count.',
65: 'CPOL determines the idle level of SCK; the two panels illustrate different sampling phases. Data changes on one clock edge and is sampled on the other. Both devices must agree on the mode, otherwise one may sample during a transition rather than after the bit has settled.',
71: r'8N1 carries eight payload bits in ten transmitted bit-times: one start, eight data, one stop. Thus at 115200 bit/s, the ideal continuous payload rate is $115200/10=11520$ bytes/s before higher-level protocol overhead. The start edge lets the receiver align its local sampling clock for each frame.',
79: 'Apparent wording error: during a read, the master is the receiver and sends ACK after bytes it wants to continue receiving; it sends NACK after the final byte. The bullet assigning the final non-acknowledgment to the slave reverses that role. See [NXP’s I2C specification, §3.1.6](https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/user-guide/UM10204.pdf).',
80: 'A common use of the write phase is to select a device register, then read its value after the repeated START. The bus remains occupied across the direction change. The final read-byte NACK is generated by the master, as explained on page 79; the same wording issue occurs here.',
}
ISSUES['Microcontrollers'] = 'Comments flag ratiometric-reference terminology, the capture/single-shot diagram label, and the final I2C read acknowledgment role; numeric product ranges are historical.'

DECK_TITLES['Microprocessor_short_en'] = 'Microprocessors'
TOPICS['Microprocessor_short_en'] = 'Processor selection, CISC/RISC, superscalar execution, VLIW, DSPs, network processors, and MCUs.'
REVIEWED['Microprocessor_short_en'] = list(range(1,69))
TITLES['Microprocessor_short_en'] = {1:'Embedded Systems — Microprocessors',3:'Microprocessors — Introduction',19:'Microprocessors — General Purpose Processors',21:'GPP — CISC',28:'GPP — RISC',34:'Superscalarity',38:'GPP — CISC/RISC',41:'GPP — EPIC/VLIW',47:'GPP — CISC/VLIW',49:'GPP — Comparison',50:'Microprocessors — Application Specific',52:'ASP — Digital Signal Processor',59:'ASP — Network Processors',65:'ASP — Microcontrollers'}
COMMENTS['Microprocessor_short_en'] = {
7: 'The discontinuity comes from crossing the processor’s native word width. A dedicated adder can be widened by one bit; software must represent and combine multiple words, including a carry between them. The quoted penalty is illustrative: it depends on the instruction set, operand representation, and the costs included in the comparison.',
12: r'IPC and CPI are reciprocals for the same execution: $\mathrm{IPC}=I/C$ and $\mathrm{CPI}=C/I$. Execution time is $t=I\,\mathrm{CPI}/f$. For one million instructions at CPI 2 and 100 MHz, $t=20$ ms. Comparing IPC alone also misses how many instructions each ISA needs for the same task.',
13: r'The ratios point in opposite directions: lower mW/MHz is preferable, whereas higher MIPS/mW is preferable. Under a fixed workload, energy is $E=P\,t$; a lower-power processor can still consume more energy if it runs sufficiently longer. Frequency-normalized power is therefore a selection aid rather than a complete energy estimate.',
23: 'In the table, `M(a)` means the contents of memory at address `a`, while a register name denotes its current contents. Address calculation and data access are separate ideas: a base-plus-offset load computes the address from a register and an offset, then reads memory there. The final row shows `pop` despite repeating the “Push” label.',
31: '“Load/store architecture” means arithmetic operates on register values and dedicated instructions move values between memory and registers. It does not mean every arithmetic instruction reloads its operands: an intermediate result can stay in registers across many operations, reducing memory traffic.',
36: 'Several execution units allow independent instructions to overlap. The reorder buffer and commit stage explain how results can become architecturally visible in program order even when execution finishes out of order. Dependencies still constrain which instructions may run together; extra ALUs alone do not guarantee a proportional IPC increase.',
43: 'The eight slots correspond to different kinds of execution resources. Independent operations can occupy different slots in the same word, but two dependent operations cannot simply be packed together. If the compiler cannot find a useful operation for a slot, that slot may be unused, so the wide word does not guarantee eight useful results.',
49: 'Read the columns as the lecture’s simplified implementation families. Earlier slides distinguish instruction-set style (CISC/RISC), issue organization (superscalar), and compiler scheduling (VLIW); these are different design dimensions. The table’s typical IPC and power entries summarize those examples rather than defining all processors with a given ISA.',
54: r'For the dot product $2\cdot4+3\cdot5$, start with accumulator $z_0=0$: the first MAC gives $z_1=8$ and the second gives $z_2=23$. A MAC combines multiplication with accumulation; feeding it still requires operand bandwidth, which motivates the memory discussion on page 56.',
63: 'Follow a packet upward from a PHY through its channel processor to the shared buses and buffers. Several channels can process traffic in parallel, while the RISC core handles supervision. Shared buffers, lookup tables, and SDRAM can still become common bottlenecks even when the per-channel processing is independent.',
}
ISSUES['Microprocessor_short_en'] = 'Original simplified architecture comparisons retained; comments qualify performance metrics and table interpretations.'

DECK_TITLES['PLL+basics'] = 'PLL basics'
TOPICS['PLL+basics'] = 'PLL feedback, capture and lock, and programmable frequency synthesis.'
REVIEWED['PLL+basics'] = list(range(1,14))
TITLES['PLL+basics'] = {1:'Phase-Locked Loop (PLL) — basic concepts',11:'Programmable Phase-Locked Loop Clock Generator',13:'Integer-N Frequency Synthesizers with Prescalers'}
COMMENTS['PLL+basics'] = {
5: r'Equal frequencies allow a constant phase difference: $d(\phi_1-\phi_2)/dt=2\pi(f_1-f_2)=0$. That constant phase error can generate the DC control voltage needed to keep the VCO at the required frequency. Thus “locked” does not necessarily mean that the two waveforms have identical phase.',
8: r'The detector compares the input with the divided feedback, so lock requires $f_i=f_{\mathrm{out}}/N$. Rearranging gives $f_{\mathrm{out}}=Nf_i$: dividing inside the feedback loop produces multiplication at the VCO output. Here $f_o$ labels the feedback/input frequency; it is distinct from the free-running-frequency notation on page 6.',
10: r'At lock, $f_{\mathrm{in}}/R=f_{\mathrm{out}}/N$, hence $f_{\mathrm{out}}=f_{\mathrm{in}}N/R$. With the diagram’s 10 MHz crystal, $R=5$ and $N=24$ give 48 MHz. At fixed $R$, increasing $N$ by one changes the output by $f_{\mathrm{in}}/R$, subject to the device’s valid operating ranges.',
}

DECK_TITLES['Programmable+Timers+-+introduction'] = 'Programmable Timers - introduction'
TOPICS['Programmable+Timers+-+introduction'] = 'Timer/counter modes, 8051 registers, prescaling, interrupts, and hardware-triggered acquisition.'
REVIEWED['Programmable+Timers+-+introduction'] = list(range(1,15))
TITLES['Programmable+Timers+-+introduction'] = {6:'Using Timers and Counters to Create Efficient MCU-based Designs'}
COMMENTS['Programmable+Timers+-+introduction'] = {
2: 'The divide-by-12 and divide-by-24 limits in the comparison table belong to the classic 8051 example developed on pages 3–5. The general distinction is the source of counted events: an internal periodic clock measures elapsed time, whereas an external input counts events. Other MCU timer clocks need not use those divisors.',
4: 'Apparent table-label error: the second mode-bit column is headed “M2,” while the text and TMOD layout on page 3 identify the pair as M1 and M0. Read the four rows as M1:M0 = 00, 01, 10, and 11.',
7: r'Prescaling trades resolution for range. Dividing a 1 MHz clock by 1000 gives 1 ms per timer tick. For the illustrated counter that reloads after counting through zero, values $M,M-1,\ldots,0$ span $M+1$ ticks, so $T=(M+1)/f_t$. Check the particular timer’s terminal-count convention before programming a delay.',
11: 'The timer determines when sampling begins; DMA moves the completed conversion into RAM. Software need only handle a completed buffer instead of timing every sample or copying each value. This separates sample timing from interrupt-service delays, provided conversion and transfer finish quickly enough for the next trigger.',
}
ISSUES['Programmable+Timers+-+introduction'] = 'Comment flags the M2/M0 table heading; 8051-specific divisors and timer count convention are qualified.'

DECK_TITLES['The+role+of+the+IDE'] = 'The role of the IDE'
TOPICS['The+role+of+the+IDE'] = 'Cross-development, vendor IDE examples, debugging probes, trace, and virtual targets.'
REVIEWED['The+role+of+the+IDE'] = list(range(1,23))
TITLES['The+role+of+the+IDE'] = {1:'How to program a microcontroller — Role of the IDE',2:'Needs — Rationale behind a microcontroller',5:'MDK-Professional',6:'MDK — Some of the main features',9:'Verification tools — Parasoft C/C++test',10:'Verification tools — Parasoft',13:'Code Composer Studio (CCS)',15:'Deep analysis of execution — Trace analysis',17:'System Analyzer',18:'Linux Development',19:'IAR Systems',20:'IAR for RISC-V — Evaluation Kit',22:'Do not forget that… — Linux'}
COMMENTS['The+role+of+the+IDE'] = {
2: 'The host is the computer running the development tools; the target is the MCU that executes the resulting program. Cross-compilation generates instructions for the target even when the compiler runs on a different architecture. A probe or simulation model then connects the host debugger to that target.',
15: 'A breakpoint shows state after stopping at a chosen event. Trace instead preserves a history of execution, which helps explain the path leading to an intermittent failure. The buffer/receiver path in the slide determines how much history can be retained; it is separate from the application’s ordinary data path.',
20: 'Source limitation: some text in this evaluation-kit slide is clipped at the right edge in the supplied PDF. The full PDF page is preserved in the image; words outside the source page cannot be recovered from this rendering.',
}
ISSUES['The+role+of+the+IDE'] = 'Page 20 has right-edge text clipping in the source. Product features and interfaces are the supplied historical examples, not a current tool-selection guide.'

DECK_TITLES['More+on+Breakpoints+and+watchpoints'] = 'More on Breakpoints and watchpoints'
TOPICS['More+on+Breakpoints+and+watchpoints'] = 'Two-page reading on hardware/software breakpoints and data watchpoints.'
REVIEWED['More+on+Breakpoints+and+watchpoints'] = [1,2]
TITLES['More+on+Breakpoints+and+watchpoints'] = {1:'Debugging, breakpoints and watchpoints',2:'Breakpoints and watchpoints — continuation'}
COMMENTS['More+on+Breakpoints+and+watchpoints'] = {2:'If a variable has an unexpected value and you do not know which instruction changed it, a write watchpoint on its memory location can stop at the modifying access. A code breakpoint is useful when you already know the instruction location to inspect. The available trigger types depend on the target’s debug hardware.'}

DECK_TITLES['Watchdog+timers+general+introduction'] = 'Watchdog timers general introduction'
TOPICS['Watchdog+timers+general+introduction'] = 'Timeout detection, recovery stages, safe outputs, independent watchdogs, and a smart-watchdog example.'
REVIEWED['Watchdog+timers+general+introduction'] = list(range(1,24))
TITLES['Watchdog+timers+general+introduction'] = {9:'Fail-safe systems in multistage watchdogs',10:'Use of two sets of control states — Runmode and Safemode',11:'Two-stage Watchdog to recover normal operating mode',13:'Fault detection and corrective actions',19:'Increase robustness with external watchdogs',20:'Designing a smart watchdog solution for IoT',23:'Plenty of features that could be added to the sample code'}
COMMENTS['Watchdog+timers+general+introduction'] = {
2: 'The timeout only tests whether the expected servicing event happened in time. A faulty program that continues kicking the watchdog can escape detection. This is why page 18 ties servicing to functional progress and page 20 adds communication monitoring instead of relying solely on a heartbeat.',
12: 'The stages start sequentially. If their timeout intervals are T1, T2, and T3, a persistent failure reaches system reset after approximately T1 + T2 + T3 from the last valid kick. Switching to safe outputs happens at T1, so protection of outputs does not wait for the final processor reset.',
15: 'Apparent bit-name typo: the final sentence repeats “WDTE.” The two controls named earlier on the slide are WDIE (interrupt enable) and WDE (reset enable); these are the pair relevant to the combined mode.',
19: 'The drawing keeps VDD connected and drives a Reset line. That connection illustrates a processor reset; it does not itself show supply power cycling. Actual power cycling would require control over the supply path. Read the power-cycle bullet as a possible system-level recovery arrangement, not a consequence of every external watchdog reset.',
22: 'Treat this as illustrative code. `Reset=false` records a heartbeat; the foreground periodically tests it and arms the next check. The initialization configures P1.1 as an input but enables an interrupt on P2.0, so those lines do not identify the same pin. The next slide also states that servicing the external TPL5010 is still to be added.',
}
ISSUES['Watchdog+timers+general+introduction'] = 'Comments flag the repeated enable-bit name, reset/power-cycle distinction, and inconsistent heartbeat pin in the illustrative code; page 23 explicitly lists unfinished example features.'

COMMENTS['Microprocessor_short_en'][29] = 'Terminology correction: RISC expands to “Reduced Instruction Set Computer,” rather than “Restricted.” The discussion concerns simplifying individual instructions and their implementation. See [Arm’s definition](https://www.arm.com/glossary/risc).'
COMMENTS['Microprocessor_short_en'][33] = 'The statement that RISC architectures have no hardware for pipeline conflicts is too broad. It describes a simplified implementation model, not a restriction imposed by a RISC instruction set. For a concrete counterexample, [Arm documents out-of-order scheduling in Cortex-A72](https://support.arm.com/documentation/100095/0002/introduction/product-documentation-and-design-flow/documentation). Keep ISA style separate from microarchitecture when reading this comparison.'
ISSUES['Microprocessor_short_en'] += ' Comments also flag the expansion of RISC and the blanket pipeline-hardware claim.'

DECK_TITLES['Over+the+Air+Firmware+Update'] = 'Over the Air Firmware Update'
TOPICS['Over+the+Air+Firmware+Update'] = 'OTA update architecture, second-stage bootloaders, memory staging, and firmware authentication.'
REVIEWED['Over+the+Air+Firmware+Update'] = list(range(1,39))
COMMENTS['Over+the+Air+Firmware+Update'] = {
9: 'Separate the destination address from the bytes to store there. In the example, half of each eight-byte packet is address overhead and half is firmware data; the packet address is not itself written as an instruction. This is an illustrative packet format, not a required OTA format.',
13: 'The upper drawing is memory organization; the lower drawing is execution flow after reset. The table of contents supplies the application choice, allowing the SSBL to remain the stable entry point while different application images occupy other regions. “User space” here labels MCU memory accessible to the application, rather than an operating-system privilege level.',
15: 'The description is deliberately incomplete: branching to a reset handler does not reproduce all effects of a hardware reset. The next pages explain why the old RTOS and interrupt state matter. Compare [[University/Embedded Systems/Notes/2 From processors to microcontrollers-20261026/Boot of a system#Slide 33 — Bootloader – With IVT offset register|Boot, page 33]] for the additional vector-table, stack, and interrupt steps.',
17: 'A fixed SSBL protects the path by which control reaches the chosen application. It does not by itself make every update step immune to power loss: application storage and the table of contents still need recoverable updates. This qualifies the broad power-cycle claim here using the failure-recovery requirement on pages 10 and 36.',
23: 'The 2 kB SRAM block is a temporary mirror of one flash page, rather than a second complete application image. Page-based buffering reduces the amount of staging RAM, but the implementation must track which bytes of the current page have arrived before writing it and reusing the buffer.',
27: 'Two qualifications: a fixed-size hash is collision-resistant, not mathematically unique; and an unauthenticated digest can be replaced together with the data. The signatures introduced on page 29 supply the missing authenticity check. See [NIST’s hash-function properties](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/hash_function).',
29: 'The operation drawn is digital signing and verification. The private key signs, and the client uses a trusted public key to verify; this does not encrypt the payload for confidentiality. That distinction is also made by the separate signing and encryption items on page 34.',
31: r'Read the table’s “+” as concatenation of bytes, not numeric addition. For example, authenticate $H_0$ with the initial signature; then check $H(P_1\Vert H_1)=H_0$ before trusting $H_1$ for the next packet. The server builds the dependencies backwards from a defined final value. The terminal value is not specified here, so this is a conceptual scheme rather than a complete packet protocol.',
36: 'The two rollback statements need a policy distinction. Recovery returns to an authorized known-good image after a failed installation; downgrade prevention blocks installing an older vulnerable release as an ordinary update. The recovery mechanism and version policy must be designed together so that recovery does not bypass the intended version restriction.',
}
ISSUES['Over+the+Air+Firmware+Update'] = 'Comments qualify reset handover, power-loss claims, hash uniqueness/authenticity, signature terminology, and rollback policy. Hash-chain termination and some implementation details are not supplied by the deck.'

DECK_TITLES['2021 hardware technologies'] = '2021 hardware technologies'
TOPICS['2021 hardware technologies'] = 'IC fabrication and packaging, custom design, programmable logic families, and FPGA structure and implementation.'
REVIEWED['2021 hardware technologies'] = list(range(1,64))
TITLES['2021 hardware technologies'] = {1:'Embedded Systems — Hardware Technologies for ICs',23:'Programmable and Reprogrammable Technologies'}
COMMENTS['2021 hardware technologies'] = {
10: 'The cross-sections show selective processing: the mask defines which regions are exposed, so later removal or modification acts only where intended. Read pages 10–12 as successive stages of one example, not as unrelated completed devices. Page 13 explicitly identifies this example as bipolar-transistor fabrication rather than a complete CMOS process.',
20: 'A standard cell is already characterized and laid out for a particular fabrication technology. The designer combines these cells instead of drawing every transistor. A common cell height permits rows; routing then connects pins across those rows. “Manhattan geometry” refers to horizontal and vertical routing directions.',
29: 'The stored SRAM bit controls a physical switch between wires. Loading configuration bits therefore changes the circuit’s connectivity; it is different from sending ordinary program instructions to a fixed CPU. Since SRAM is volatile, this configuration must be restored after power loss unless another mechanism stores it persistently.',
34: 'Apparent acronym swap: the expansions of PAL and PLA are reversed in this list. Pages 37, 39, and 40 consistently give PLA = Programmable Logic Array, and PAL = Programmable Array Logic. Use those later diagrams to distinguish which AND/OR planes are programmable.',
38: r'With $n$ input bits, the decoder generates $2^n$ possible minterms, one per address. Each of the $m$ output bits selects the minterms for which it should be 1. A ROM implementing a three-input, four-output truth table therefore stores $2^3\times4=32$ bits.',
39: r'A PLA need not generate every minterm. For $f=ab+a\bar c$, two AND terms are sufficient: connect $a,b$ to one product line and $a,\bar c$ to another, then connect both products to the output OR. Unlike a full minterm, a product term can omit an input.',
43: 'The output-select multiplexer can choose a combinational result or the stored flip-flop value. Feeding a registered output back into the logic supplies the current state for a finite-state machine. The clock advances that state; the combinational AND/OR network computes outputs and next-state values between clock edges.',
53: r'A LUT4 stores the truth table of a four-input Boolean function: $2^4=16$ configuration bits. The input pattern selects one stored result. The surrounding multiplexers choose signal paths and whether an output bypasses the flip-flop or is registered, so a logic block supports both combinational and sequential circuits.',
55: 'Synthesis converts HDL behavior into a network of logic resources. Place-and-route assigns those resources to actual FPGA locations and wires; the bitstream encodes the resulting configuration. Functional simulation checks behavior, while timing analysis checks whether signal paths satisfy constraints such as the requested clock period.',
57: 'The RAM and shift-register uses are alternative configurations of a SLICEM LUT, rather than extra resources simultaneously available beside that same LUT’s full logic function. Resource totals in the next tables must therefore be read with the chosen configuration in mind.',
}
ISSUES['2021 hardware technologies'] = 'Comment flags swapped PLA/PAL expansions; the slide families and resource tables describe the supplied historical technology examples.'

DECK_TITLES['2021 PCB based design'] = '2021 PCB based design'
TOPICS['2021 PCB based design'] = 'PCB components and packages, board layers, fabrication data, assembly, and boundary-scan testing.'
REVIEWED['2021 PCB based design'] = list(range(1,45))
TITLES['2021 PCB based design'] = {1:'Embedded Systems — Architectures and Design Styles (part 1 — PCB based design)',3:'Architectures and approaches to the design',27:'Information to provide when ordering PCBs',37:'Do it by yourself — large components/large pitch'}
COMMENTS['2021 PCB based design'] = {
19: r'The unit °C/W identifies thermal resistance, rather than thermal conductivity. For a specified thermal path, the temperature rise is approximately $\Delta T=\theta P$. A path rated at 40 °C/W produces a 20 °C rise at 0.5 W; the temperatures compared must match that resistance’s stated endpoints.',
21: 'Copper on different layers remains electrically separate unless connected intentionally. The enlarged via shows a conducting barrel joining selected layers; gaps around it prevent contact with planes that belong to other nets. A multilayer drawing therefore represents both routing space and controlled electrical connections through the stack.',
25: 'A schematic symbol describes electrical terminals; its footprint defines where physical pads and holes go on the board. Correct logic in the schematic cannot compensate for the wrong footprint or an incorrect pin-to-pad mapping. Check package variant and pin 1 orientation together when interpreting the land pattern.',
27: 'The copper “weight” specifies copper quantity per unit area and is used to indicate layer thickness; it is not the total mass of copper on the finished board. The manufacturing data also separates layer count, trace spacing, hole dimensions, and surface finish because each constrains a different fabrication step.',
29: 'A through via spans the board; a blind via connects an outer layer to an internal layer; a buried via connects internal layers without opening at the surfaces. In the cross-section, trace the white conducting regions to see which copper layers each via actually joins.',
35: 'Read the profile as a sequence of material changes rather than a single oven temperature. Early heating prepares the paste and flux; melting forms the joints; controlled cooling solidifies them. The graph is an illustrative process profile for the supplied material, rather than one setting applicable to every solder alloy and component.',
40: 'CTE means coefficient of thermal expansion. When the board, component, and solder expand by different amounts during a temperature cycle, the joints experience mechanical strain. This explains why matching materials and joint geometry matter even after a board passes its initial electrical tests.',
44: 'The thin serial path threads through boundary cells around several chips; it is separate from their ordinary board interconnections. Test bits enter at TDI and leave at TDO. By loading drivers and capturing receivers, the test can inspect connections whose package pins are difficult to reach with physical probes.',
}

COMMENTS['2021 hardware technologies'][6] = 'Apparent corporate-history error: “Fairchild Semiconductor, today ST” conflates different company histories. Fairchild was acquired by ON Semiconductor in 2016; ST was formed by combining SGS and Thomson semiconductor businesses in 1987. See [onsemi’s acquisition letter](https://www.onsemi.com/site/pdf/Fairchild-Customer-Letter.pdf) and [ST’s history](https://investors.st.com/resources/faqs).'
ISSUES['2021 hardware technologies'] += ' Page 6 corporate history is explicitly qualified with primary sources.'

DECK_TITLES['SOC, Distributes ESs, Prototyping platforms'] = 'SOC, Distributes ESs, Prototyping platforms'
TOPICS['SOC, Distributes ESs, Prototyping platforms'] = 'SoC partitioning and interconnects, design for testability, distributed systems, and prototyping platforms.'
REVIEWED['SOC, Distributes ESs, Prototyping platforms'] = list(range(1,50))
TITLES['SOC, Distributes ESs, Prototyping platforms'] = {1:'Embedded Systems — Architectures and Design Styles',45:'Example for development of apps on DSP (TMDXEVM6455)'}
COMMENTS['SOC, Distributes ESs, Prototyping platforms'] = {
9: 'Putting data near each computing block reduces traffic to a shared memory, but copies or ownership then need coordination. For example, one accelerator must finish writing its result before another consumes it. Centralized memory simplifies locating shared data, while multiple simultaneous accesses can contend for the same resource.',
13: 'Here “sw” denotes a network switch. A transfer passes from its processing element through switches and links toward the destination. Independent transfers can use different parts of the mesh concurrently, but transfers sharing a link can contend. The topology supplies possible routes; it does not by itself guarantee latency or consistency.',
19: 'The instruction register selects a test operation; the selected data register then lies on the serial path from TDI to TDO. BYPASS shortens that path through an unneeded device, while EXTEST targets external interconnections. Keep the control signals TMS/TCK distinct from the serial test-data signals TDI/TDO.',
21: 'The TPG supplies stimuli to the unit under test (UUT); the TRE/ORA evaluates responses under the BIST controller. “Signature based” here means compacting a sequence of test responses into a value for comparison. It is a test-analysis concept, distinct from the public-key digital signatures discussed in the OTA deck.',
26: 'Apparent wording error: the example calls CAN a “wireless” connection, but the automotive discussion refers to cabling and page 27 draws nodes on a shared bus. Read this CAN example as a wired field bus; it belongs with the wired automotive category on page 24.',
32: 'The local SPI/I2C links connect components within a sensor node, such as its MCU and radio chip. The wireless link connects separate nodes. These are different levels of communication: choosing a radio protocol does not determine how the MCU communicates with that radio inside the node.',
40: 'HAL means hardware abstraction layer; BSP means board support package. They provide software support for the target hardware and board wiring so the application can use the available peripherals. Their presence reduces bring-up work, but the application still needs to be verified with the actual board configuration.',
48: 'A working prototype validates selected behavior on the chosen platform. Moving to a product can change the board, package, power distribution, timing, or available resources. The final verification step therefore concerns the product implementation as well as the prototype’s application logic.',
}
ISSUES['SOC, Distributes ESs, Prototyping platforms'] = 'Comment flags the CAN wireless/wired wording; prototype products and tool claims are historical examples.'

COMMENTS['SOC, Distributes ESs, Prototyping platforms'][45] = 'Apparent processor-type error: the slide calls the TMS320C6455 a floating-point DSP, but TI identifies it as a fixed-point C64x+ DSP. The quoted instruction throughput should therefore not be read as floating-point operations per second. See [TI’s device description](https://www.ti.com/product/TMS320C6455).'
ISSUES['SOC, Distributes ESs, Prototyping platforms'] += ' Page 45 fixed-point/floating-point classification is qualified using TI documentation.'

DECK_TITLES['Case study FPGA vs ASIC'] = 'Case study FPGA vs ASIC'
TOPICS['Case study FPGA vs ASIC'] = 'Feasibility study for replacing an FPGA-based UMTS subsystem with a mixed-signal ASIC, including area, packaging, NRE, and volume economics.'
REVIEWED['Case study FPGA vs ASIC'] = list(range(1,37))
COMMENTS['Case study FPGA vs ASIC'] = {
6: r'Apparent conversion inconsistencies: using $1\ \mathrm{KiB}=1024$ bytes, $1290752$ ROM bits give $157.5625$ KiB, rather than the stated 179.5 Kbyte. Likewise, $16\times1024$ FIFO bits give 2 KiB, rather than 2.5. The RAM figure, $61440/8/1024=7.5$ KiB, is consistent. The slide alone does not establish whether the bit totals or the converted totals were intended.',
8: 'Equivalent gate count is an estimate of the implemented logic, not a direct count of FPGA configuration bits or an assurance of equal performance. The study explicitly reduces the estimated ASIC count from the FPGA equivalent. The later technology-density calculation uses the 2.5-million-gate ASIC estimate, while memory and analog area are budgeted separately.',
12: 'The axes compare sample rate with resolution. The power curves show the tradeoff at approximately constant dissipation: higher speed at the same resolution, or higher resolution at the same speed, generally moves toward a higher-power region of this chart. Use the required operating point to narrow candidates; the chart is not one ADC’s transfer characteristic.',
15: 'The package budget includes power, ground, clocks, test access, and probes as well as functional signals. The listed groups sum to 210 connections, so a nominal 256-pin package leaves room beyond that estimate. This is why counting only the 104 digital signals would underestimate the required package.',
20: r'For the 0.18 µm estimate, $2.5\times10^6/(70\times10^3)\approx35.7\ \mathrm{mm}^2$ gives the digital-logic area, matching the rounded 36 in the table. Analog and RAM areas are then added. The resulting die area therefore cannot be inferred from digital gate density alone.',
28: 'Cost-boundary ambiguity: page 27 retains a 36 USD PCB contribution and estimates about 12 USD savings from reducing the layer count. Here the wording appears to remove the full additional 36 USD. Keep those two assumptions separate when reproducing the comparison; the slides do not reconcile them.',
29: r'Apparent unit error: the text says a die area of $60\ \mathrm{cm}^2$, while the preceding estimate is roughly $8\times7\ \mathrm{mm}^2$. The table is consistent with about $60\ \mathrm{mm}^2=0.6\ \mathrm{cm}^2$: at $30\ \mathrm{USD}/\mathrm{cm}^2$, 30,000 dies cost 540,000 USD, and adding about 759,000 USD NRE gives the displayed 1.30 million USD. Read the printed area unit with this qualification.',
30: r'With a fixed development cost $C_0$ and per-unit costs $c_{\mathrm{COTS}}$ and $c_{\mathrm{ASIC}}$, break-even satisfies $C_0+Nc_{\mathrm{ASIC}}=Nc_{\mathrm{COTS}}$. Thus $N=C_0/(c_{\mathrm{COTS}}-c_{\mathrm{ASIC}})$. For the study’s 759,000 USD NRE, 230 USD COTS cost, and 18 USD ASIC unit cost, this is about 3,580 units. The result changes with the PCB-cost assumption flagged on page 28.',
}
ISSUES['Case study FPGA vs ASIC'] = 'Comments flag memory-size conversions, the PCB-cost boundary, and the die-area unit. Original historical estimates and all feasibility qualifications are retained.'

DECK_TITLES['PCB Design with Altium - Eagle_WF_2019'] = 'PCB Design with Altium - Eagle_WF_2019'
TOPICS['PCB Design with Altium - Eagle_WF_2019'] = 'Historical Altium and EAGLE walkthroughs from schematic capture to PCB routing, rule checks, 3D views, and manufacturing outputs.'
REVIEWED['PCB Design with Altium - Eagle_WF_2019'] = list(range(1,75))
COMMENTS['PCB Design with Altium - Eagle_WF_2019'] = {
8: r'A mil is one thousandth of an inch, so the 100 mil grid corresponds to 2.54 mm. The snap grid constrains where objects can be placed; changing the visible grid alone need not change that placement constraint. Distinguish this schematic spacing from the later PCB dimensions and routing rules.',
9: 'The library symbol supplies logical pins; the footprint supplies physical pads on the board. Their pin-to-pad mapping connects these two representations. See [[University/Embedded Systems/Notes/3 Design of Hw Systems-20261026/2021 PCB based design#Slide 25 — Footprints|PCB based design, page 25]] for why a correct symbol still needs the correct package footprint.',
18: 'A net label names an electrical connection; it does not create a voltage source or impose the displayed voltage. Labeling the supply net “12V” identifies the intended supply connection. The actual voltage comes from the circuit and the connected source.',
26: 'The thin connection lines show required connections from the schematic before copper has been routed. They guide placement but are not conducting tracks. Compare page 37, where the routed copper paths replace those connection requirements with physical geometry.',
32: r'Via diameter and hole size are different dimensions. With a 1 mm outer diameter and a 0.6 mm hole, the nominal radial copper ring is $(1-0.6)/2=0.2$ mm before manufacturing tolerances. This is the copper surrounding the drilled hole, not the hole diameter itself.',
40: 'DRC checks the layout against the enabled geometric and connectivity constraints. A clean report means those selected checks pass; it does not establish that the schematic implements the intended function. The rule scopes and limits configured on the preceding pages determine what the checker can report.',
47: r'In the shown millimeter format, 4:3 allocates four integer digits and three fractional digits, giving a coordinate increment of 0.001 mm. The drill and Gerber data must describe the same physical coordinates, so consistent units, format, and origin matter when the files are combined by the manufacturer.',
57: 'Source limitation: the screenshot overlaps part of instruction 4 in the supplied PDF. That part of the instruction is unreadable in the original page; the image preserves the overlap without inventing the missing words.',
64: 'The routing percentage describes how much of the required connectivity the routing attempt completed; the via count describes one aspect of its geometry. A 100% result still needs the design-rule check on the next page. Neither number alone evaluates signal behavior or whether the chosen placement is suitable.',
}
ISSUES['PCB Design with Altium - Eagle_WF_2019'] = 'Page 57 instruction 4 is partly covered by a screenshot in the source. Small raster screenshots retain their original softness. Menus, licenses, prices, and product comparisons remain historical course examples.'

